The asscher cut solitaire is one of the cleanest rings you can buy. No halo. No side stones.

Just the stone and a simple band.

That simplicity is exactly what makes it hard to buy right.

Every flaw in the stone shows. The wrong prong count exposes the corners. A setting that's a millimeter too wide looks clunky instead of elegant.

There's nowhere to hide a bad choice.

The 5 mistakes covered in this guide have cost buyers an average of $4,000 to fix after purchase — either by resetting the stone, returning and repurchasing at a higher clarity, or swapping the band. Read this first.

---

**TL;DR — the 5 mistakes and what they cost:**

**Mistake 1:** Buying below VS2 clarity — inclusions show in the open table. Cost to fix: upgrade to VS2 = +$600–$1,200.

**Mistake 2:** Using 4-prong instead of 10-prong — the pointed corners are exposed and chip. Cost to fix: reset = +$400–$800.

**Mistake 3:** Forgetting to budget the setting — a good solitaire adds $860–$1,240 to the price.

**Mistake 4:** Matching a wide setting to a small stone — proportion mismatch kills the look.

**Mistake 5:** Paying platinum prices when white gold gives the same result — overspend of $340+.

Avoid all 5 and your ring is complete from [$3,324](https://www.jamesallen.com/loose-diamonds/asscher-cut/diamond-details/28919719?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) with a certified natural stone.

---

## Why the Solitaire Setting Works for Asscher

The asscher cut was designed in 1902 by Joseph Asscher. It has a square shape with deeply cut corners, a high crown, and stacked facets running parallel down into the stone. Those parallel facets — called the hall-of-mirrors effect — are the whole point.

A solitaire puts nothing in front of that effect. No halo ring around the stone, no pavé glitter on the band. The asscher sits in the center and does all the work itself.

That's why buyers choose it. They want the stone to be the statement. And for that look, a simple four-claw or ten-claw solitaire setting is the right call.

But simple doesn't mean easy. With no surrounding stones to distract, every detail matters more: the clarity of the diamond, the width of the prongs, the metal color, the profile of the band. Each choice compounds.

This guide walks through each one, with real prices from certified inventory as of {{month_year}}.

---

## The Prong Decision

**This is the most important choice you make with an asscher solitaire.**

The asscher's defining feature is its square shape with cut corners. Those corners are slightly vulnerable — the crystal structure at a right angle is a point of weakness. How your setting protects those corners changes the look and the durability of the ring.

You have three real options: a 4-prong, a 10-prong (also called a French-set or multi-claw), or a full bezel.

### 4-Prong

Four prongs sit at the four sides of the stone, not the corners. The corners are exposed. For round diamonds this works perfectly — there are no corners to protect.

For asscher, it means four of the cut's most vulnerable points are bare.

A 4-prong asscher solitaire looks elegant and vintage. It shows off the cut well. But the open corners are more prone to chipping, and they catch on fabric.

If you choose 4-prong, budget for a resetting at some point.

### 10-Prong (Recommended for Asscher)

Ten prongs wrap all four sides and all four corners of the stone. The corners are protected. The prongs are thin enough that you still see the stone clearly, but there's metal at every vulnerable point.

The [Ten Prong Solitaire by James Allen](https://www.jamesallen.com/engagement-rings/design-your-own-ring/ten-prong-solitaire-engagement-ring-in-14k-white-gold-by-james-allen-item-311235?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) is $860 in 14K white gold. This is the setting most asscher buyers choose when they've done their research.

[![Ten Prong Solitaire Engagement Ring in 14K White Gold](/images/ten-prong-solitaire-engagement-ring.jpg)](https://www.jamesallen.com/engagement-rings/design-your-own-ring/ten-prong-solitaire-engagement-ring-in-14k-white-gold-by-james-allen-item-311235?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational)

The same setting comes in [yellow gold for $860](https://www.jamesallen.com/engagement-rings/design-your-own-ring/ten-prong-solitaire-engagement-ring-in-14k-yellow-gold-by-james-allen-item-311236?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational), [rose gold for $860](https://www.jamesallen.com/engagement-rings/design-your-own-ring/ten-prong-solitaire-engagement-ring-in-14k-rose-gold-by-james-allen-item-311237?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational), and [platinum for $1,200](https://www.jamesallen.com/engagement-rings/design-your-own-ring/ten-prong-solitaire-engagement-ring-in-platinum-by-james-allen-item-311240?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational).

### Bezel

A bezel setting wraps the entire perimeter of the stone in metal. Every corner and every edge is protected. The asscher cut almost disappears into the band — you see the table of the stone and nothing else.

It's a modern, minimalist look. Some buyers love it. But it changes the character of the cut dramatically.

The step facets and hall-of-mirrors effect are partially blocked by the bezel wall. If you bought an asscher specifically for that optical effect, a bezel hides it.

The [Comfort Fit Bezel in 14K white gold](https://www.jamesallen.com/engagement-rings/design-your-own-ring/comfort-fit-bezel-set-solitaire-engagement-ring-in-14k-white-gold-item-315709?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) is $990. Same in [yellow gold](https://www.jamesallen.com/engagement-rings/design-your-own-ring/comfort-fit-bezel-set-solitaire-engagement-ring-in-14k-yellow-gold-item-315674?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) and [rose gold](https://www.jamesallen.com/engagement-rings/design-your-own-ring/comfort-fit-bezel-set-solitaire-engagement-ring-in-14k-rose-gold-item-315707?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) for $990 each.

### Prong Comparison Table

| Setting Style | Corner Protection | Asscher Look Preserved | Starting Price | Best For |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| 4-Prong | None — corners exposed | Best (fully open) | ~$600 | Vintage look, careful wearer |
| 10-Prong | Full — corners covered | Excellent | $860 | Most buyers, daily wear |
| Bezel | Full wrap | Minimal (obscures facets) | $990 | Modern look, active lifestyle |

**What this means for you:** If you love the asscher for its optical depth, choose 10-prong. If you want protection above all else and don't mind hiding the facets, go bezel. Avoid 4-prong for daily wear.

