## Metal Guide for Asscher Solitaires

Metal is a real decision for asscher cut rings. The stone's color and the metal's color interact directly — with no halo or side stones to break up the visual field.

### White Gold (14K)

White gold is the most popular choice. It makes the diamond look whiter — any color the stone carries blends into the metal and disappears. An F-color stone in a white gold solitaire reads as colorless.

The drawback is rhodium plating. White gold is naturally a light grey-gold color. It's plated with rhodium to appear white.

That plating wears off over 2–3 years and the ring needs replating ($50–$80 at any jeweler). It's routine maintenance, but maintenance nonetheless.

14K white gold is harder than platinum and more scratch-resistant for daily wear.

### Yellow Gold (14K)

Yellow gold works beautifully with asscher cuts that have an H–I color grade. The warm metal color draws the eye and makes any slight warmth in the stone indistinguishable.

Vintage asscher rings from the Art Deco period were almost exclusively set in platinum and yellow gold. A yellow gold ten-prong solitaire carries that original character.

If you're at F–G color or above, yellow gold may introduce a faint warmth around the stone's edge. At H color, it's invisible — buy H and save on the stone.

### Platinum

Platinum is the original asscher metal. In the 1920s, platinum was the status metal and asscher cuts were made for it. Platinum is 95% pure (vs 58.5% for 14K gold), denser, heavier, and naturally white — no plating required.

The Ten Prong in platinum costs [$1,200](https://www.jamesallen.com/engagement-rings/design-your-own-ring/ten-prong-solitaire-engagement-ring-in-platinum-by-james-allen-item-311240?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) vs $860 for 14K white gold — a $340 premium. For daily wear, the durability difference is real: platinum develops a patina rather than scratching, and the metal never needs replating.

For most buyers: 14K white gold is the right choice. For buyers who want permanence and don't mind the weight and cost: platinum.

### Rose Gold

Rose gold pairs unusually well with asscher cuts. The warm metal tones add contrast against the step facets' depth. It's unconventional — an asscher in rose gold stands out from the typical Art Deco look.

The Ten Prong in [14K rose gold is $860](https://www.jamesallen.com/engagement-rings/design-your-own-ring/ten-prong-solitaire-engagement-ring-in-14k-rose-gold-by-james-allen-item-311237?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational). Worth considering if you want something distinctive.

| Metal | Maintenance | Price Premium | Best For |
|---|---|---|---|
| 14K White Gold | Replating every 2–3 years | — | Most buyers, G–H color stones |
| 14K Yellow Gold | None | — | H–I color stones, vintage aesthetic |
| Platinum | None | +$340 | Permanence seekers, D–F color |
| 14K Rose Gold | None | — | Distinctive look, H–I color |

**What this means for you:** Go white gold unless you have a specific reason not to. Save the $340 platinum premium and put it toward a better stone.

